const t=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"time-texts-soon-near","title":"The time texts: 'soon', 'near', 'at hand'","question":"Do the New Testament's time indicators bind the fulfilment of its prophecies to the first-century audience?","topic":"second-coming","summary":"Revelation opens and closes by saying its events are 'soon' and 'near', and Jesus and the apostles use similar language. Partial preterists take these as temporal markers pointing to AD 70; futurists read them as expressions of imminence measured on God's timescale; amillennial and inaugurated-eschatology interpreters read them as the already/not-yet nearness of the last days begun at Christ's resurrection.","positions":["partial-preterism","futurism","amil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"partial-preterism","text":"The New Testament's time indicators (Revelation 1:1, 1:3, 22:6, 22:10; Matthew 16:28; James 5:8-9; 1 Peter 4:7) were addressed to real first-century audiences and should be taken at face value. Revelation 22:10 deliberately reverses Daniel 8:26 and 12:4: Daniel sealed his vision because its fulfilment was distant, while John is told not to seal his because 'the time is near'. Preterists such as Gentry, DeMar and Sproul argue that the most natural fulfilment of the bulk of these 'near' prophecies is Christ's covenantal coming in judgment on Jerusalem and the temple in AD 70, which preserves the plain force of the words for their first readers.","passages":["Revelation 1:1-3","Revelation 22:6-10","Daniel 12:4","Matthew 16:28"],"sources":["demar-last-days-madness","gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","sproul-last-days"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"futurism","text":"Futurists answer that the phrase en tachei in Revelation 1:1 and 22:6 can describe the manner of events (rapidly, suddenly once they begin) rather than their nearness to John. Prophetic nearness is also measured on God's timescale, which 2 Peter 3:8-9 explicitly addresses in the face of scoffers who complained of delay. Since Revelation's content includes a bodily return, final judgment and new creation (Revelation 19-22) that plainly did not occur in AD 70, the time words cannot by themselves determine the fulfilment of the whole book.","passages":["Revelation 1:1","2 Peter 3:8-9","Revelation 21:1"],"sources":["gentry-ice-great-tribulation","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Preterists reply that Revelation 1:3 and 22:10 use engys ('near'), a plainly temporal word that cannot be explained as manner, and that the instruction not to seal the book is explicitly about timing. 2 Peter 3:8 concerns God's patience, not a redefinition of the words he chose to comfort persecuted readers. Partial preterists also do not claim that all of Revelation was fulfilled: Gentry locates the near prophecies chiefly in chapters 4-19 and sees chapters 20-22 reaching beyond AD 70 to the consummation.","passages":["Revelation 1:3","Revelation 22:10"],"sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","gentry-ice-great-tribulation"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"futurism","text":"Critics observe that once partial preterists grant that the 'soon' language of Revelation 22:6-12 stands beside promises of a final coming still future to them (22:12, 22:20 'I am coming soon'), the principle that time words govern all adjacent content is weakened. If 22:20 can speak of a Second Coming that is still unfulfilled, the futurist appeal to God's perspective applies equally to 1:1. Futurists therefore read the time texts as expressing imminence and urgency rather than a calendar.","passages":["Revelation 22:12","Revelation 22:20"],"sources":["gentry-ice-great-tribulation","gregg-revelation-four-views"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Amillennial and inaugurated-eschatology readers (Beale, Hoekema) agree the time texts are real but argue that they express the already/not-yet structure of New Testament eschatology. The last days began with Christ's resurrection and Pentecost (Acts 2:17, Hebrews 1:2), so the end is always 'near' in redemptive-historical terms. Revelation 1:1 echoes Daniel 2:28-29, signalling that what Daniel placed in 'the latter days' has begun, without restricting fulfilment to a single event such as AD 70.","passages":["Acts 2:17","Hebrews 1:2","Daniel 2:28-29"],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Preterists grant the inaugurated framework but argue it does not exhaust the texts: Jesus ties specific events to specific hearers (Matthew 10:23, Matthew 16:28, Matthew 26:64), and the destruction of the temple is the one great public event within that horizon that the Olivet discourse names. Treating 'near' as a permanent quality of the entire age, they argue, means no prophecy could ever be 'not near', emptying the words of content for the original readers.","passages":["Matthew 10:23","Matthew 26:64"],"sources":["sproul-last-days","wright-jesus-victory"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Beale replies that his reading is not timeless: the allusion to Daniel 2 in Revelation 1:1 is precisely a first-century claim that the eschatological era has dawned. Amillennialists add that identifying Revelation's prophecies chiefly with AD 70 depends on an early date for the book, which is disputed, whereas the inaugurated reading works on either date.","passages":["Revelation 1:1","Daniel 2:28-29"],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"this-generation-genea","title":"'This generation' (Matthew 24:34)","question":"Who is the 'generation' that will not pass away until 'all these things' take place?","topic":"olivet-generation","summary":"Matthew 24:34 is a hinge text. Dispensational futurists read it of the future generation that sees the signs; partial preterists read it of Jesus' contemporaries and place the events of 24:4-34 in AD 70; many amillennial and evangelical commentators take it of Jesus' contemporaries while distinguishing the signs from the Second Coming itself.","positions":["disppremil","partial-preterism","amil","futurism"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"disppremil","text":"Matthew 24:34 means that the generation alive when 'all these things' (the signs of 24:4-31, including the abomination of desolation, the unparalleled tribulation and the visible coming of the Son of Man) begin to occur will not pass away before they are completed. Since Christ's return with cosmic signs and the worldwide gathering of the elect (24:29-31) did not occur in AD 70, Thomas Ice argues the generation must be the future one that witnesses the signs. The fig-tree parable immediately before (24:32-33) speaks of those who 'see all these things', defining the generation by what it sees.","passages":["Matthew 24:32-34","Matthew 24:29-31"],"sources":["ice-matthew-24-this-generation","gentry-ice-great-tribulation","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Every other use of 'this generation' (he genea haute) in Matthew refers to Jesus' contemporaries (Matthew 11:16, 12:41-45, 23:36). Gentry stresses that Matthew 23:36, 'all these things will come upon this generation', immediately precedes the discourse, uses nearly identical wording and plainly targets the scribes and Pharisees; the disciples' question (24:3) arises from Jesus' prediction of the temple's ruin (24:2). The 'coming on the clouds' imagery of 24:29-31 draws on Old Testament judgment oracles against nations (Isaiah 13:10, Isaiah 19:1), fitting the fall of Jerusalem within forty years.","passages":["Matthew 23:36","Matthew 24:2-3","Isaiah 13:10","Isaiah 19:1"],"sources":["gentry-key-fob-olivet","gentry-ice-great-tribulation","demar-last-days-madness"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Ice answers that 23:36 concerns the judgment that fell on that generation for rejecting the Messiah, whereas 24:34 refers to the generation that sees the end-time signs; context, not a lexical default, sets the referent. Some interpreters also propose that genea can mean 'race' or 'kind', so that the Jewish people will survive until the end. Futurists further argue that the cosmic signs, the coming 'as lightning' visible everywhere (24:27) and the gathering from the four winds (24:31) are too universal to be exhausted by Rome's siege of Jerusalem.","passages":["Matthew 24:27","Matthew 24:30-31"],"sources":["ice-matthew-24-this-generation","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Preterists respond that most lexicons and commentators reject the 'race' sense for these Synoptic texts, and that Jesus' repeated 'you' to his disciples (24:4, 24:6, 24:15, 24:33) assumes they will witness the signs. They read 24:30 with Daniel 7:13 as the Son of Man's vindication, which Jesus told the high priest he would 'see' (Matthew 26:64). Gentry, Sproul and France place a transition at 24:36 ('that day and hour'), so the Second Coming is distinguished, not denied.","passages":["Daniel 7:13","Matthew 24:36","Matthew 26:64"],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","sproul-last-days","wright-jesus-victory"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Many amillennial and evangelical commentators hold that 'this generation' does mean Jesus' contemporaries, but that 'all these things' refers to the preliminary signs (wars, persecution, false messiahs, the temple's fall) rather than to the parousia itself, whose 'day and hour' no one knows (24:36). That generation would see everything needed to make the end imminent, so Matthew 24:34 neither requires a distant future generation nor collapses the Second Coming into AD 70.","passages":["Matthew 24:34-36","Matthew 24:4-14"],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists reply that this split view must still explain why 24:29 places the cosmic signs and coming 'immediately after the tribulation of those days'. If the tribulation was AD 70, either the Son of Man came then or 'immediately' must stretch across two millennia. The futurist reading keeps 'immediately' natural by placing both tribulation and coming in the same future generation.","passages":["Matthew 24:29"],"sources":["ice-matthew-24-this-generation","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists answer that prophetic telescoping is common, with Old Testament oracles blending near and far horizons, and that 'the tribulation of those days' can describe the whole period of distress inaugurated by Jerusalem's fall (compare 'the times of the Gentiles' in Luke 21:24). Luke's parallel explicitly locates the desolation in Jerusalem surrounded by armies (Luke 21:20), anchoring at least part of the discourse in the first century, which a wholly future reading struggles to accommodate.","passages":["Luke 21:20-24"],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"revelation-date-early-late","title":"The date of Revelation: before AD 70 or c. AD 95?","question":"Was Revelation written under Nero (before AD 70) or late in Domitian's reign (c. AD 95)?","topic":"revelation-date","summary":"The date is disputed. Most modern commentators favour c. AD 95 on the strength of Irenaeus and other early witnesses; partial preterists argue for a date in the mid-60s from internal evidence. The question matters most for preterism, since a post-70 date makes a prophecy of Jerusalem's fall unlikely; many amillennial, idealist and eclectic readings do not depend on it.","positions":["partial-preterism","eclectic","futurism"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Partial preterists such as Gentry argue that Revelation was written in the mid-60s, before AD 70; the question is disputed, and most modern scholars favour c. AD 95. John is told to measure the temple and altar while the outer court is trampled for forty-two months (Revelation 11:1-2), implying the temple still stands. The seven kings of 17:10, of whom 'five have fallen, one is', fit the Julio-Claudian line with Nero as the sixth, and 666 (13:18) matches a Hebrew spelling of 'Nero Caesar'. Gentry also stresses the silence about Jerusalem's destruction, the most momentous event imaginable for a Jewish-Christian prophet had it already happened. On an early date, the book's 'soon' prophecies can refer to the Jewish War.","passages":["Revelation 11:1-2","Revelation 17:10","Revelation 13:18"],"sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","gregg-revelation-four-views"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"eclectic","text":"Late-date scholars such as Beale reply that the temple of 11:1-2 is symbolic of God's people, as temple language is elsewhere (1 Corinthians 3:16, Revelation 3:12), so it says nothing about a standing building. The count of kings in 17:10 depends on where one starts and whether the three brief emperors of 68-69 are included, and many read the seven as a symbolic totality. The 666 identification needs a particular Hebrew spelling, and silence about AD 70 is weak evidence in a book written in symbols.","passages":["Revelation 3:12","1 Corinthians 3:16"],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","pate-four-views-revelation"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Early-date advocates answer that a purely symbolic temple does not explain why the outer court is handed to the nations for a specific forty-two months, roughly the length of Rome's campaign (AD 67-70). They also note that the variant reading 616 in some manuscripts matches the Latin form of Nero's name, suggesting early scribes understood a Nero reference. In their view, coherent internal evidence should outweigh one ambiguous patristic statement.","passages":["Revelation 11:2","Revelation 13:18"],"sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","gregg-revelation-four-views"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"eclectic","text":"Late-date scholars reply that forty-two months is Daniel's stock period for the persecution of God's people (Daniel 7:25, 12:7), which Revelation uses repeatedly (11:2-3, 12:6, 12:14, 13:5) as a symbol rather than as the length of a military campaign. A Nero reference behind 666 or 616 would not by itself fix an early date: Beale and Bauckham read the beast's healed mortal wound (13:3; 17:8-11) against the legend of Nero's return that circulated after his death, which if anything suits a later setting.","passages":["Daniel 7:25","Revelation 12:6","Revelation 13:3"],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","bauckham-theology-revelation"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"futurism","text":"Futurists, with most modern commentators, date Revelation to about AD 95, near the end of Domitian's reign. The earliest explicit witness is Irenaeus (Against Heresies 5.30.3, c. AD 180), who knew Polycarp, a disciple of John, and says the apocalyptic vision was seen toward the end of Domitian's reign; Clement of Alexandria, Victorinus and Eusebius agree. Internally, the spiritual decline of Ephesus and the complacent wealth of Laodicea (Revelation 2:4, 3:17) suit a later date. If so, Revelation cannot be primarily a prophecy of the AD 70 destruction.","passages":["Revelation 2:4","Revelation 3:17","Revelation 1:9"],"sources":["irenaeus-against-heresies","beale-revelation-nigtc","gentry-ice-great-tribulation"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Gentry contends that Irenaeus' testimony is ambiguous: the verb heorathe ('it/he was seen') may refer to John himself being seen in Domitian's time rather than to the vision. Irenaeus made other demonstrable chronological errors (for example, that Jesus lived to nearly fifty), some later traditions (the title of the Syriac version, a statement in Epiphanius) place John's exile under Claudius or Nero, and later fathers largely depend on Irenaeus, so the multiple attestation is less independent than it appears. Scholarly majorities have also shifted before: in the nineteenth century Westcott, Hort, Lightfoot and Farrar favoured a Neronic date.","passages":["Revelation 1:9"],"sources":["gentry-before-jerusalem-fell","gregg-revelation-four-views"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"futurism","text":"Late-date scholars, futurist and non-futurist alike (Beale among them), reply that most patristic scholars and commentators judge 'the apocalyptic vision' to be the natural subject of the verb, since Irenaeus' point is how recently the Revelation was given, and the old Latin translation reads it that way. The alternative traditions are late or confused, whereas the Domitianic date is attested by several writers. They add that the internal arguments are themselves contested, so they cannot simply override the earliest external testimony.","passages":[],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","irenaeus-against-heresies"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"literal-symbolic-hermeneutic","title":"Literal or Christ-centred fulfilment? The prophetic hermeneutic","question":"Should Old Testament promises to Israel be fulfilled in the way their original hearers understood them, or as the New Testament reinterprets them in Christ?","topic":"kingdom","summary":"Underneath most millennial disagreements lies a hermeneutical one. Dispensationalists defend consistent 'normal' interpretation of prophecy; covenant theologians argue the New Testament's own use of the Old Testament must govern; progressive covenantalists stress typological escalation across the covenants. Progressive dispensationalism has narrowed some of the gaps.","positions":["dispensationalism","covenant-theology","progressive-covenantalism"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"dispensationalism","text":"Dispensationalists hold that consistent grammatical-historical interpretation, applied to prophecy as to any other genre, guards against subjective spiritualising; Ryrie lists it among the sine qua non of dispensationalism. 'Literal' here allows figures of speech but means that promises made to Israel about land, throne and nation (Jeremiah 31:35-37, Ezekiel 36-37, Amos 9:11-15) will be fulfilled in the sense their first hearers would have understood. First-advent prophecies were fulfilled literally (Micah 5:2, Zechariah 9:9), which Walvoord and Pentecost take as the pattern for the second.","passages":["Jeremiah 31:35-37","Amos 9:11-15","Micah 5:2","Zechariah 9:9"],"sources":["ryrie-dispensationalism","pentecost-things-to-come","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"covenant-theology","text":"Covenant theologians argue the New Testament is the authoritative guide to reading the Old. James applies Amos 9:11-12 to the inclusion of Gentiles in the church (Acts 15:15-18), Paul identifies Abraham's seed as Christ and those in him (Galatians 3:16, 3:29), and Hebrews presents the land and city as pointing to a heavenly country (Hebrews 11:10, 11:16). Poythress argues that 'literal' is an ambiguous term and that original audiences already heard prophecy in typological, covenantal categories; Christ-centred fulfilment is reading as the apostles read, not spiritualising.","passages":["Acts 15:15-18","Galatians 3:16","Galatians 3:29","Hebrews 11:16"],"sources":["poythress-understanding-dispensationalists","hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"dispensationalism","text":"Dispensationalists, especially progressive ones such as Blaising and Bock, answer that New Testament fulfilment can be complementary rather than replacing. Including Gentiles in Acts 15 does not cancel the rebuilding of David's house for Israel, and Paul still says the promises belong to Israel (Romans 9:4) and that 'all Israel will be saved' because God's gifts and calling are irrevocable (Romans 11:26-29). Widening a promise's beneficiaries is not the same as reassigning it.","passages":["Romans 9:4","Romans 11:26-29"],"sources":["blaising-bock-progressive","vlach-church-replaced-israel"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"covenant-theology","text":"Reformed critics reply that the 'both/and' approach still has to retain features, such as restored temple sacrifices in Ezekiel's vision (Ezekiel 43:18-27), that Hebrews declares obsolete (Hebrews 8:13, 10:18). They note that dispensationalists themselves concede symbolic elements in prophecy, so the real question is not literal versus symbolic but which reading is controlled by the apostles' own usage.","passages":["Ezekiel 43:18-27","Hebrews 8:13","Hebrews 10:18"],"sources":["poythress-understanding-dispensationalists","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"progressive-covenantalism","text":"Progressive covenantalists (Peter Gentry and Stephen Wellum) argue that the covenants unfold through escalating typology. Israel, land, temple and David are types that reach their antitype in Christ, the true Israel (Matthew 2:15, John 15:1) and true temple (John 2:19-21), and the land promise expands to the whole renewed earth (Romans 4:13, Matthew 5:5). Reading prophecy 'literally' without this escalation, they argue, misses the Bible's own storyline.","passages":["Matthew 2:15","John 2:19-21","Romans 4:13","Matthew 5:5"],"sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"dispensationalism","text":"Dispensationalists respond that typology does not cancel the type: Christ as the true Israel can restore national Israel just as Christ as the true man redeems actual human beings. Vlach argues that the prophets repeatedly picture a restored Israel among the nations in the age to come (Isaiah 19:24-25, Isaiah 60), and that Acts 1:6-7 and Matthew 19:28 assume rather than deny a future restoration.","passages":["Isaiah 19:24-25","Acts 1:6-7","Matthew 19:28"],"sources":["vlach-church-replaced-israel","blaising-bock-progressive"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"progressive-covenantalism","text":"Gentry and Wellum reply that the issue is not whether ethnic Jews have a future in Christ (many in their camp affirm a future turning of Israel from Romans 11) but whether Israel as a nation retains a distinct covenant role after Christ fulfils the covenants. They read Acts 1:6-8 as Jesus redirecting the disciples from a national timetable to a worldwide witness.","passages":["Acts 1:6-8","Romans 11:25-27"],"sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"recapitulation-revelation-19-20","title":"Recapitulation or sequence in Revelation 19-20?","question":"Does Revelation 20:1 continue the story after Christ's return in 19:11-21, or begin a new vision that goes back to the start of the church age?","topic":"millennium","summary":"If 20:1-10 follows 19:11-21 chronologically, the millennium comes after the Second Coming (premillennialism). If it recapitulates, the thousand years describe the present age (amillennialism, and many postmillennialists). The debate turns on Revelation's structure, the battle scenes and the nations.","positions":["amil","histpremil","disppremil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"amil","text":"Revelation is built of parallel sections that each span the time between Christ's comings (Hendriksen's 'progressive parallelism'), and 20:1 begins a new section that returns to the start of the church age. R. Fowler White notes that the battle of 20:7-10 is the same as that of 19:17-21 and 16:14-16: each is called 'the war' (ton polemon), each draws on Ezekiel 38-39's Gog-Magog imagery, and each gathers the kings of the whole earth. If these are one final battle, 20:1-6 must precede it and describe the present age.","passages":["Revelation 16:14-16","Revelation 19:17-21","Revelation 20:7-10","Ezekiel 39:17-20"],"sources":["white-recapitulation-rev20","hendriksen-more-than-conquerors","beale-revelation-nigtc"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"histpremil","text":"Premillennialists see a continuous narrative: the rider of 19:11-21 defeats the beast and false prophet and throws them into the lake of fire, and then ('and I saw', kai eidon, 20:1) an angel binds Satan. Revelation 20:10 throws the devil into the lake 'where the beast and the false prophet were', presupposing their earlier consignment in 19:20. Ladd argued that sealing Satan 'so that he might not deceive the nations any longer' (20:3) fits naturally after the nations' armies have been defeated at Christ's return.","passages":["Revelation 19:20","Revelation 20:1-3","Revelation 20:10"],"sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists reply that 'and I saw' introduces new visions throughout Revelation without implying chronological order. The note that the beast and false prophet 'were' already in the lake can reflect the order of John's visions rather than of events. And if the nations were destroyed in 19:18-21 ('all people, both free and slave, small and great'), no nations remain for Satan to deceive in 20:3 and 20:8, a problem White presses hard against the sequential reading.","passages":["Revelation 19:18","Revelation 20:3","Revelation 20:8"],"sources":["white-recapitulation-rev20","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"Premillennialists answer that apocalyptic 'all' is often hyperbolic in Revelation (compare 6:15, 13:16), and that survivors from the nations may enter the kingdom (compare Zechariah 14:16). Harold Hoehner argued that recapitulation must treat the binding of 20:1-3 as parallel to the casting down of 12:9, yet the two differ: in one Satan is thrown to earth and rages, in the other he is shut in the abyss and cannot deceive.","passages":["Revelation 12:9","Zechariah 14:16"],"sources":["white-hoehner-recapitulation","ladd-commentary-revelation"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists argue that Revelation's structure is progressive, not cyclical: the seventh seal contains the trumpets and the seventh trumpet leads to the bowls (8:1-2, 11:15, 15:1), and 19:11-22:5 forms a single closing section (return, millennium, last judgment, new creation), each scene introduced by 'and I saw'. Deere and Walvoord argue that reading 20:1-6 back into the church age requires an unannounced leap across two thousand years.","passages":["Revelation 8:1-2","Revelation 15:1","Revelation 19:11","Revelation 21:1"],"sources":["deere-premil-rev20","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Beale counters that the seals, trumpets and bowls themselves show repetition: the sixth seal (6:12-17), seventh trumpet (11:15-19) and seventh bowl (16:17-21) each reach the final judgment, so the book arrives at the end several times. A return to the beginning at 20:1 fits a pattern already established, as 12:1-5 jumps back to Christ's birth and ascension.","passages":["Revelation 6:12-17","Revelation 11:15-19","Revelation 12:1-5"],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","hendriksen-more-than-conquerors"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Premillennialists respond that recapitulation elsewhere does not prove it here; each case needs textual signals, and they find none at 20:1 comparable to the clear restart at 12:1. They add that the sequence of a thousand years followed by Satan's release 'after these things' (meta tauta, 20:3) assumes historical progression.","passages":["Revelation 20:3","Revelation 12:1"],"sources":["deere-premil-rev20","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"one-or-two-resurrections","title":"One general resurrection or two?","question":"Does Scripture teach one resurrection of the just and unjust at Christ's return, or two bodily resurrections separated by the millennium?","topic":"first-resurrection","summary":"Premillennialists read Revelation 20:4-6 as two bodily resurrections a thousand years apart. Amillennialists and postmillennialists, with the Reformed confessions, hold to a single general resurrection, appealing to John 5:28-29, Daniel 12:2 and Acts 24:15, and to Paul's tie between the resurrection and the destruction of death.","positions":["histpremil","amil","postmil","disppremil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"histpremil","text":"Revelation 20:4-6 describes two bodily resurrections separated by a thousand years: those who 'came to life and reigned with Christ' and 'the rest of the dead' who did not come to life until the thousand years ended. Paul's ordered resurrection, Christ the firstfruits, then at his coming those who belong to him, 'then the end' (1 Corinthians 15:23-24), leaves room for an interval, and Luke 14:14 ('the resurrection of the righteous') and Philippians 3:11 (a resurrection 'out from among' the dead) hint at a resurrection of believers distinct from the general one. Ladd held that later revelation can disclose an interval earlier texts did not specify.","passages":["Revelation 20:4-6","1 Corinthians 15:23-24","Luke 14:14","Philippians 3:11"],"sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","blomberg-chung-historic-premil","grudem-systematic"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Jesus says 'an hour is coming' when all who are in the tombs will hear his voice and come out, the good to a resurrection of life and the evil to a resurrection of judgment (John 5:28-29); Daniel 12:2 and Acts 24:15 likewise speak of one resurrection of righteous and wicked together. Jesus repeatedly places the raising of believers 'on the last day' (John 6:39-40, 44, 54), and Matthew 25:31-46 sets the judgment of all nations at a single coming. Hoekema and Riddlebarger argue the clearer didactic texts should govern one highly symbolic vision.","passages":["John 5:28-29","Daniel 12:2","Acts 24:15","John 6:39-40"],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","storms-kingdom-come"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"Premillennialists reply that 'hour' (hora) in John need not be a single moment: the 'hour' that 'now is' in John 5:25 has lasted the whole church age, so the resurrection 'hour' of 5:28 can span a thousand years. Prophecy often compresses events later separated, as Isaiah 61:1-2 joins the two advents and Jesus stops reading mid-sentence (Luke 4:18-21). Some also read Daniel 12:2 as distinguishing 'some' raised to life from 'others' to shame.","passages":["John 5:25","Isaiah 61:1-2","Luke 4:18-21"],"sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists answer that John 5:25 describes a spiritual raising and 5:28 a bodily one, so the elasticity of the first 'hour' cannot simply be transferred to the second. Believers are raised 'on the last day' (John 6:40) and unbelievers are judged 'on the last day' (John 12:48); placing a thousand years between them strains the phrase. They also note that the 'some... others' reading of Daniel 12:2 is a minority view among Hebrew scholars.","passages":["John 6:40","John 12:48"],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists such as Boettner and Gentry, with the Westminster Confession (32.3) and its catechisms, affirm one general resurrection of just and unjust at Christ's single return at the end of history. Paul's 'then comes the end' (1 Corinthians 15:24) follows directly on Christ's coming, Christ reigns now 'until' the last enemy, death, is destroyed (15:25-26), and death is swallowed up at the very resurrection of believers (15:54). That leaves no room for death to continue through a millennium after believers are raised.","passages":["1 Corinthians 15:24-26","1 Corinthians 15:54"],"sources":["westminster-confession","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","boettner-millennium"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"Premillennialists argue that 'then' (eita) in 15:24 belongs to a sequence that already contains a long gap between Christ's resurrection and his coming (15:23), so a further interval between the coming and 'the end' fits Paul's wording. On this reading the reign 'until' all enemies are subdued (15:25) culminates in Christ's messianic kingdom on earth after the parousia.","passages":["1 Corinthians 15:23-25"],"sources":["blomberg-chung-historic-premil","bock-three-views"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists answer that the gap after the firstfruits is implied by Christ's ascension, whereas Paul gives no hint of a millennial gap. 15:54-55 ties the defeat of death to the resurrection at the last trumpet, so no death-bearing millennium can follow it. Gentry and Mathison also read the reign of 15:25 as Christ's present heavenly session (Psalm 110:1, Acts 2:34-36), already under way.","passages":["1 Corinthians 15:54-55","Psalm 110:1","Acts 2:34-36"],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"ezesan-came-to-life","title":"'They came to life' (ezesan) in Revelation 20:4-5","question":"Is the 'first resurrection' of Revelation 20:4-5 a bodily resurrection or the believer's present, spiritual life with Christ?","topic":"first-resurrection","summary":"The same verb, ezesan, describes the saints who 'came to life' (20:4) and the rest of the dead who did not 'come to life' until the thousand years ended (20:5). Premillennialists insist both are bodily; amillennialists (and many postmillennialists) read the first as the soul's life with Christ in heaven or as regeneration, citing the 'first/second' contrast in Revelation.","positions":["amil","histpremil","disppremil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"amil","text":"Revelation 20:4 says the souls of the martyrs 'came to life' (ezesan) and reigned with Christ. Kline, Hoekema and Beale take this 'first resurrection' as the believer's entrance into heavenly life in the intermediate state. Kline argues that 'first' (protos) in Revelation marks the present, provisional order in contrast to the final one (21:1, 'the first heaven and the first earth had passed away'), so the first resurrection differs in kind from the bodily resurrection, just as the first (physical) death differs from the second death in the lake of fire (20:14).","passages":["Revelation 20:4-6","Revelation 21:1","Revelation 20:14"],"sources":["kline-first-resurrection","hoekema-bible-future","beale-revelation-nigtc"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"histpremil","text":"Premillennialists since Henry Alford press a grammatical point: the same verb ezesan is used of the saints in 20:4 and of 'the rest of the dead' in 20:5. If the second is a bodily resurrection, consistency requires the first to be bodily too; reading one spiritually and the other literally in the same context, Alford argued, leaves language without fixed meaning. The same aorist describes Christ's bodily resurrection in Revelation 2:8 ('who died and came to life').","passages":["Revelation 20:5","Revelation 2:8"],"sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","deere-premil-rev20","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Kline and Hoekema reply that John himself signals a qualitative contrast with 'first' and 'second': the text names a first resurrection and a second death, implying a non-bodily first resurrection paired with a non-final first death. John 5:24-29 shows the same pattern, with the dead who 'hear and live' spiritually now and a bodily resurrection to come. The 20:5 statement is also negative ('did not come to life'), so the parallel is looser than claimed.","passages":["John 5:24-29"],"sources":["kline-first-resurrection","kline-first-resurrection-reaffirmation","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"J. Ramsey Michaels, responding to Kline in the Westminster Theological Journal, accepted that 'first' can signal a difference in kind but argued it does not follow that the first resurrection is non-bodily. Premillennialists add that the 'souls of those beheaded' echoes Revelation 6:9-11, where the martyrs' souls wait for vindication; 20:4 grants that vindication by narrating a change of state, which is what resurrection means.","passages":["Revelation 6:9-11"],"sources":["michaels-first-resurrection-response","ladd-commentary-revelation"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists such as Deere and Walvoord argue that 'resurrection' (anastasis, 20:5-6) almost everywhere in the New Testament means bodily resurrection. The martyrs' reward of reigning answers the promise to overcomers of authority over the nations (2:26-27) and the song that the redeemed 'will reign on the earth' (5:10). A purely heavenly intermediate state, they argue, does not fulfil a promise of reigning on the earth.","passages":["Revelation 2:26-27","Revelation 5:10"],"sources":["deere-premil-rev20","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Beale replies that the saints' reign is already partly realised (Revelation 1:6, 'he made us a kingdom') and that the intermediate state is participation in Christ's present heavenly reign (Ephesians 2:6). 'Resurrection' in 20:5-6 is qualified by 'first', marking it as distinct, and the thrones of 20:4, like the thrones elsewhere in Revelation, are in heaven.","passages":["Revelation 1:6","Ephesians 2:6"],"sources":["beale-revelation-nigtc","storms-kingdom-come"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Premillennialists respond that Ephesians 2:6 describes the living believer's positional union with Christ, not the post-mortem reward given specifically to martyrs, and that John's sequence (souls of the beheaded, then they 'came to life') narrates an event at a point in time. They regard the amillennial reading as importing categories from elsewhere into an otherwise natural sequence.","passages":["Revelation 20:4"],"sources":["deere-premil-rev20","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"binding-of-satan-now-or-future","title":"The binding of Satan: now or future?","question":"Was Satan bound at Christ's first coming, or does Revelation 20:1-3 describe a future, total confinement?","topic":"satan-binding","summary":"Amillennialists and postmillennialists hold that Satan was bound at the first advent so that he can no longer prevent the gospel from reaching the nations. Premillennialists argue that the language of Revelation 20:1-3 describes a complete removal from earthly activity that has not yet happened.","positions":["amil","postmil","disppremil","histpremil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists (Riddlebarger, Hoekema, Storms), with postmillennialists, hold that Satan was bound at Christ's first coming. Jesus described his exorcisms as binding the strong man in order to plunder his house (Matthew 12:28-29, using the same verb deo as Revelation 20:2), saw Satan fall like lightning (Luke 10:18), and announced that the ruler of this world would be cast out as he drew all people to himself (John 12:31-32). The binding in Revelation 20:3 has a specific purpose: Satan can no longer deceive the nations as before (Acts 14:16, Acts 17:30), so the gospel goes to the Gentiles.","passages":["Matthew 12:28-29","Luke 10:18","John 12:31-32","Revelation 20:1-3"],"sources":["riddlebarger-binding-satan","riddlebarger-case-amil","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"disppremil","text":"Premillennialists argue the binding of Revelation 20 is far more than a restriction: Satan is seized, chained, thrown into the abyss, which is then shut and sealed over him (20:1-3). Yet the New Testament describes Satan in the church age as prowling like a roaring lion (1 Peter 5:8), blinding unbelievers' minds (2 Corinthians 4:4) and at work in the disobedient (Ephesians 2:2). Waymeyer concludes that a partial, present restraint does not match the totality of John's language.","passages":["1 Peter 5:8","2 Corinthians 4:4","Ephesians 2:2"],"sources":["waymeyer-binding-satan","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists respond that apocalyptic imagery depicts spiritual realities; the chain and the abyss are no more literal than the dragon himself. The binding has one stated purpose, 'so that he might not deceive the nations', and does not claim to end all satanic activity, just as a dog on a long chain is restrained without being idle. The worldwide spread of the gospel since Pentecost (Matthew 28:18-20) is the evidence of this restriction.","passages":["Revelation 20:3","Matthew 28:18-20"],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil","hendriksen-more-than-conquerors"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Premillennialists reply that 2 Corinthians 4:4, Revelation 12:9 and 13:14 describe Satan's present work precisely as deception, including of 'the whole world', the very activity 20:3 says is halted. They also argue that the strong-man saying concerns Jesus' authority over demons in his ministry, not a sealed confinement lasting through the church age.","passages":["Revelation 12:9","Revelation 13:14"],"sources":["waymeyer-binding-satan","deere-premil-rev20"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"histpremil","text":"Historic premillennialists such as Ladd grant that Christ won a decisive victory over Satan at the cross, but argue that Revelation 20 depicts a further stage. Revelation 12 portrays Satan thrown down to the earth in great wrath (12:12); 20:1-3 portrays him removed from the earth altogether. Because these are distinct events in John's sequence, the first-advent victory texts do not exhaust 20:1-3.","passages":["Revelation 12:12","Revelation 20:1-3"],"sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","blomberg-chung-historic-premil"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists such as Gentry, agreeing with amillennialists on timing, note that Revelation 12 ties Satan's fall to Christ's ascension and to the saints' victory 'by the blood of the Lamb' (12:5, 12:10-11), the same first-advent setting. His raging and his inability to deceive the nations are complementary perspectives on one defeat, and the ongoing discipling of the nations (Matthew 28:19) shows the binding at work in history.","passages":["Revelation 12:5","Revelation 12:10-11","Matthew 28:19"],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"Premillennialists answer that the two visions differ in location (earth versus abyss) and effect (raging versus sealed), so one cannot easily be read as the other. They also note that Paul and John describe the present age as marked by deception (2 Thessalonians 2:9-11, 1 John 5:19), which is hard to square with nations no longer deceived.","passages":["2 Thessalonians 2:9-11","1 John 5:19"],"sources":["ladd-commentary-revelation","waymeyer-binding-satan"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"wheat-tares-postmil-progress","title":"Matthew 13: kingdom growth or wheat and tares until the end?","question":"Do the kingdom parables of Matthew 13 forecast the gospel's progressive worldwide dominance, or a mixed growth of good and evil until the harvest?","topic":"optimism","summary":"Postmillennialists read the mustard seed and leaven as pictures of the kingdom's eventual pervasive triumph in history. Amillennialists stress the wheat and tares growing together until the end of the age; some dispensationalists read the parables as describing a mixed or even corrupted professing church.","positions":["postmil","amil","disppremil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists read the kingdom parables of Matthew 13 as forecasting the gospel's progressive, worldwide growth before Christ returns. The mustard seed grows from the smallest of seeds into a tree in which the birds nest (13:31-32), echoing Daniel 4 and Ezekiel 17's image of a great kingdom sheltering the nations, and the leaven works until 'all' the dough is leavened (13:33). Read with Daniel's stone that becomes a mountain filling the whole earth (Daniel 2:35, 44), Boettner, Mathison and Gentry see a pattern of gradual, pervasive kingdom triumph within history.","passages":["Matthew 13:31-33","Daniel 2:35","Daniel 2:44","Ezekiel 17:22-24"],"sources":["boettner-millennium","mathison-postmillennialism","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists point to the parable of the wheat and the tares in the same chapter: good and evil grow together 'until the harvest', which is 'the end of the age' (13:30, 13:39-40), and the dragnet likewise gathers good and bad until the end (13:47-50). Riddlebarger and Hoekema argue this describes parallel development of good and evil throughout the age, and that the mustard seed and leaven teach the kingdom's unexpected reach and hidden power rather than a Christianised world.","passages":["Matthew 13:24-30","Matthew 13:36-43","Matthew 13:47-50"],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists reply that the tares parable teaches coexistence, not proportion. The field is the world, and the point is that the servants must not uproot the tares by force but leave separation to the final judgment; nothing says the tares stay as numerous as the wheat. Boettner and Gentry argue that evil persisting until the end is fully compatible with the gospel becoming dominant, as weeds can remain in a field that yields an overwhelming wheat harvest.","passages":["Matthew 13:38-39"],"sources":["boettner-millennium","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists respond that the parable's emphasis on both growing to the harvest, combined with warnings of increasing lawlessness and a final apostasy (Matthew 24:12, 2 Thessalonians 2:3), makes a closing golden age hard to see. Hoekema proposes a 'realistic optimism': Christ reigns and his kingdom grows now, but conflict and suffering continue until the consummation.","passages":["Matthew 24:12","2 Thessalonians 2:3"],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","storms-kingdom-come"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"disppremil","text":"Many classic dispensationalists read Matthew 13 as the 'mysteries of the kingdom' in the interadvent age, which Pentecost characterised as a mixed sphere of Christian profession. Leaven is elsewhere almost always a symbol of corruption (Matthew 16:6, 1 Corinthians 5:6-8), so it may depict false teaching spreading in Christendom, and the birds lodging in the mustard tree may recall the birds that snatch the seed (13:4, 13:19). On this reading the parables forecast a corrupted professing church, not global conversion.","passages":["Matthew 13:4","Matthew 13:19","Matthew 16:6","1 Corinthians 5:6-8"],"sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists, with most commentators, respond that the parable says 'the kingdom of heaven is like leaven', so the leaven is the kingdom itself and cannot here symbolise evil. Leavened loaves were offered to God at the feast of firstfruits (Leviticus 23:17), and a tree sheltering birds is a recognised image of a great kingdom over the nations (Daniel 4:12, Ezekiel 31:6).","passages":["Leviticus 23:17","Daniel 4:12","Ezekiel 31:6"],"sources":["mathison-postmillennialism","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Many dispensationalists, especially progressive ones, accept the positive reading of the leaven but argue the parables describe the extent of the kingdom's present form, not worldwide righteousness, and that the kingdom in its full Davidic sense awaits the King's return (Luke 19:11-15, Acts 1:6-7). The core disagreement is therefore about when the kingdom is consummated, not these images alone.","passages":["Luke 19:11-15","Acts 1:6-7"],"sources":["pentecost-things-to-come","blaising-bock-progressive"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"perilous-times-2-timothy-3","title":"'Perilous times' (2 Timothy 3) and postmillennial hope","question":"Does Paul's warning about 'difficult times in the last days' describe the whole church age, ruling out a future Christianised world?","topic":"optimism","summary":"Amillennialists and premillennialists often cite 2 Timothy 3 as a portrait of the entire last days. Postmillennialists, notably Gentry in reply to James White, read it as an occasional warning about specific false teachers whose progress Paul says will be checked; partial preterists add that the 'last days' are often the closing decades of the old covenant era.","positions":["amil","postmil","partial-preterism"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"amil","text":"Paul warns that 'in the last days difficult (chalepoi) times will come', listing people who are lovers of self, money and pleasure, holding a form of godliness while denying its power (2 Timothy 3:1-5). He adds that all who desire to live godly lives will be persecuted and that evil people and impostors 'will go on from bad to worse' (3:12-13). Since the last days span the whole age from Christ's first coming (Hebrews 1:2), amillennialists (and premillennialists) argue this characterises the entire church age, which sits uneasily with an expected Christianised world; James White has pressed this point in debate with postmillennialists.","passages":["2 Timothy 3:1-5","2 Timothy 3:12-13","Hebrews 1:2"],"sources":["gentry-james-white-2tim3","riddlebarger-case-amil","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"postmil","text":"Gentry answers that 2 Timothy is an occasional letter addressing Timothy's own situation: Paul tells Timothy to 'avoid such people' (3:5), describes those who are already creeping into households (3:6) and compares them to Jannes and Jambres. Crucially, Paul says such men 'will not get very far, for their folly will be plain to all' (3:9). Iain Murray likewise read the passage as warning about the contagion of contemporary false teachers, not forecasting inevitable decline across the age.","passages":["2 Timothy 3:5-9"],"sources":["gentry-james-white-2tim3","murray-puritan-hope"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists reply that a letter can be occasional and still describe recurring features of the last days; the present-tense commands show those days had begun, not that they would end. Paul's statement that 'all who desire to live a godly life will be persecuted' (3:12) reads as a principle for the whole age, consistent with Jesus' warnings (John 15:18-20) and Paul's own teaching that we enter the kingdom through many tribulations (Acts 14:22).","passages":["2 Timothy 3:12","John 15:18-20","Acts 14:22"],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil","storms-kingdom-come"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists agree persecution recurs but distinguish recurring periods of moral decline from a uniform downward trajectory. The 'bad to worse' of 3:13 describes the impostors' own spiral, which Paul has just said will be checked (3:9). Since the last days span the whole age, even amillennialists must allow that they include seasons of revival and reformation, which shows the text is not a forecast of the age's overall direction.","passages":["2 Timothy 3:13","2 Timothy 3:9"],"sources":["gentry-james-white-2tim3","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Partial preterists such as DeMar add that New Testament 'last days' language often refers to the final days of the old covenant order before AD 70 (compare 1 Corinthians 10:11, 1 Peter 4:7). The vices of 2 Timothy 3 and the apostasy described in Jude and 2 Peter fit the turbulent decades before Jerusalem's fall, so the passage describes a period already past rather than the church's permanent condition.","passages":["1 Corinthians 10:11","1 Peter 4:7","Jude 1:17-19"],"sources":["demar-last-days-madness","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists respond that the New Testament's last days are the age between the comings, not just the decades before AD 70; Acts 2:17 and Hebrews 9:26 present them as the eschatological era Christ inaugurated, which runs to his return. Confining 2 Timothy 3 to the pre-70 decades would also limit its function as instruction for later churches in a way the letter itself does not suggest (3:16-17).","passages":["Acts 2:17","Hebrews 9:26","2 Timothy 3:16-17"],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Preterists answer that they do not deny the passage's application to later believers, only its use as a prophecy of the age's long-term trajectory. They read 'the end of the ages' (Hebrews 9:26) and 'on whom the end of the ages has come' (1 Corinthians 10:11) as transitional texts focused on the passing of the old covenant order.","passages":["Hebrews 9:26","1 Corinthians 10:11"],"sources":["demar-last-days-madness","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"church-israel-distinction","title":"The church and Israel: distinct peoples or one?","question":"Is the church a distinct people alongside a national Israel with its own future promises, or the one people of God in which Israel's promises are fulfilled?","topic":"israel","summary":"Dispensationalists maintain a distinction between Israel and the church; covenant theologians see one people of God across the covenants; progressive covenantalists see Christ as the true Israel and the church as the new covenant people in him. Many in all camps, including Puritan postmillennialists, expect a future large-scale conversion of Jewish people from Romans 11.","positions":["disppremil","dispensationalism","covenant-theology","progressive-covenantalism"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists hold that the church and Israel are distinct peoples within God's single redemptive purpose. Paul says Israel according to the flesh still has the covenants and promises (Romans 9:4-5), distinguishes 'Jews, Greeks and the church of God' (1 Corinthians 10:32), and foresees the salvation of 'all Israel' after the fullness of the Gentiles, because God's gifts and calling are irrevocable (Romans 11:25-29). Vlach argues the church is a new entity begun at Pentecost (Matthew 16:18, Ephesians 3:5-6) and that unconditional national and land promises (Genesis 12:1-3, Jeremiah 31:35-37) remain for ethnic Israel.","passages":["Romans 9:4-5","1 Corinthians 10:32","Romans 11:25-29","Jeremiah 31:35-37"],"sources":["ryrie-dispensationalism","vlach-church-replaced-israel","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"covenant-theology","text":"Covenant theologians argue for one people of God across the covenants. Gentiles are grafted into the one olive tree (Romans 11:17-24), the dividing wall is broken down to make 'one new man' (Ephesians 2:14-16), those in Christ are Abraham's seed and heirs according to promise (Galatians 3:29), and Paul can bless 'the Israel of God' (Galatians 6:16), which many take as the whole church. The church is therefore the continuation and fulfilment of Israel, centred on the Messiah as the true Israel.","passages":["Romans 11:17-24","Ephesians 2:14-16","Galatians 3:29","Galatians 6:16"],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil","poythress-understanding-dispensationalists"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists respond that the olive tree represents covenant blessing rather than Israel's identity, and in Romans 11 the natural branches are explicitly ethnic Israel, whose regrafting is future. 'One new man' unites Jew and Gentile in salvation without erasing ethnic distinctions, just as male and female remain in Galatians 3:28. They read Galatians 6:16 ('and upon the Israel of God') as referring to believing Jews, a reading defended by scholars such as S. Lewis Johnson.","passages":["Romans 11:23-24","Galatians 3:28","Galatians 6:16"],"sources":["vlach-church-replaced-israel","blaising-bock-progressive"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"covenant-theology","text":"Covenant theologians note that many of them also expect a future mass conversion of ethnic Jews from Romans 11, a hallmark of Puritan postmillennialism traced by Iain Murray. The dispute, then, is not Israel's future salvation but whether it comes as a separate programme with distinct land and temple promises; they argue the New Testament instead extends the inheritance to the whole world (Romans 4:13).","passages":["Romans 4:13","Romans 11:26"],"sources":["murray-puritan-hope","hoekema-bible-future"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"progressive-covenantalism","text":"Progressive covenantalists (Peter Gentry and Stephen Wellum) reject both 'replacement' and a permanent Israel-church distinction: Christ is the true Israel, and the church is the new covenant people in union with him. The new covenant was promised to the house of Israel (Jeremiah 31:31-34) yet is inaugurated for the church (Luke 22:20, Hebrews 8:8-13), showing that Israel's promises are fulfilled in Christ and shared by all who are in him.","passages":["Jeremiah 31:31-34","Luke 22:20","Hebrews 8:8-13"],"sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"dispensationalism","text":"Progressive dispensationalists such as Blaising and Bock agree that the church shares in the new covenant now, but call this an initial, partial fulfilment; its national dimensions, a regathered and restored Israel (Ezekiel 36:24-28), await Christ's return. Gentile inclusion in new covenant blessings, they argue, does not require Israel's distinct promises to lapse.","passages":["Ezekiel 36:24-28"],"sources":["blaising-bock-progressive","vlach-church-replaced-israel"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"progressive-covenantalism","text":"Wellum responds that typological fulfilment in Christ is not a 'partial' fulfilment awaiting a second national stage: the land is itself a type of the new creation, and Hebrews says the patriarchs sought a better, heavenly country (Hebrews 11:16). The burden, he argues, lies on those who posit a restored national programme to show it from New Testament texts.","passages":["Hebrews 11:16"],"sources":["gentry-wellum-kingdom-covenant"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"rapture-timing","title":"The timing of the rapture","question":"Will the church be caught up before the tribulation, during it (before God's wrath), or at Christ's single return after it?","topic":"rapture","summary":"Within futurist premillennialism, pretribulationists place the rapture before Daniel's seventieth week, prewrath advocates place it within the second half before the day of the Lord's wrath, and posttribulationists identify it with the visible return after the tribulation. Amillennialists and postmillennialists generally read 1 Thessalonians 4 as describing Christ's single return, close to the posttribulational reading.","positions":["disppremil","histpremil","futurism"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"disppremil","text":"Pretribulationists hold that Christ will take the church to himself (John 14:1-3, 1 Thessalonians 4:13-18) before the seven-year tribulation of Daniel's seventieth week. Believers are 'not destined for wrath' (1 Thessalonians 5:9) and are promised to be kept 'from the hour of trial' coming on the whole world (Revelation 3:10). The rapture is imminent and preceded by no signs (Titus 2:13), whereas the coming to earth follows clear signs, implying two stages; and Walvoord and Ryrie note that the church (ekklesia) is not mentioned on earth in Revelation 4-18.","passages":["1 Thessalonians 4:13-18","1 Thessalonians 5:9","Revelation 3:10","Titus 2:13"],"sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","ryrie-dispensationalism","pentecost-things-to-come","hultberg-three-views-rapture"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"histpremil","text":"Posttribulationists (Gundry, Ladd, Moo) argue Scripture describes one return. The elect are gathered 'immediately after the tribulation' (Matthew 24:29-31) at the last trumpet (1 Corinthians 15:52), and Paul tells the Thessalonians the day of the Lord will not come until the apostasy and the man of lawlessness appear (2 Thessalonians 2:1-4). The 'meeting' (apantesis) of 1 Thessalonians 4:17 normally describes going out to welcome a visiting dignitary and escorting him back. Gundry notes that no text places the rapture before the tribulation, while several place Christ's coming and the resurrection after it.","passages":["Matthew 24:29-31","1 Corinthians 15:52","2 Thessalonians 2:1-4","1 Thessalonians 4:17"],"sources":["gundry-church-tribulation","ladd-blessed-hope","hultberg-three-views-rapture"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Pretribulationists answer that the Olivet discourse addresses Israel, so its 'elect' are tribulation saints; the 'last trumpet' of 1 Corinthians 15 is not the seventh trumpet judgment of Revelation; and 2 Thessalonians 2 reassures believers that they are not in the day of the Lord, which would be a strange comfort if they expected to endure it. Ryrie, reviewing Gundry, argued that the imminence passages are hard to reconcile with a return preceded by identifiable tribulation events.","passages":["2 Thessalonians 2:1-3","1 Corinthians 15:52"],"sources":["ryrie-review-gundry","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"Posttribulationists respond that the Olivet discourse addresses the disciples, the church's founders, whom Jesus also commissions with his presence 'to the end of the age' (Matthew 28:20). New Testament imminence, they argue, means expectancy and readiness, not that no prophesied event can precede the return; Paul himself names events that must come first (2 Thessalonians 2:3).","passages":["Matthew 28:20","2 Thessalonians 2:3"],"sources":["gundry-church-tribulation","ladd-blessed-hope"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"futurism","text":"Prewrath advocates (Rosenthal, Kurschner) agree that the church is delivered before God's wrath but deny that the whole seventieth week is wrath. They distinguish the 'great tribulation', the Antichrist's persecution of the saints (Matthew 24:21-22, Revelation 13:7), from the day of the Lord's wrath, which is announced only after the cosmic signs of the sixth seal ('the great day of their wrath has come', Revelation 6:16-17). The rapture therefore occurs in the second half of the week, after the Antichrist is revealed but before the trumpet and bowl judgments.","passages":["Matthew 24:21-22","Revelation 6:12-17","Revelation 13:7"],"sources":["rosenthal-prewrath","kurschner-antichrist-before-dol","hultberg-three-views-rapture"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Pretribulationists reply that the Lamb himself opens every seal (Revelation 6:1), so the judgments from the first seal onward are divine, and 'the wrath of the Lamb' in 6:16 can describe wrath already under way. They also argue that Daniel's seventieth week is a unit directed at Daniel's people and city (Daniel 9:24), so the church's removal belongs before it begins.","passages":["Revelation 6:1","Revelation 6:16","Daniel 9:24"],"sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come","hultberg-three-views-rapture"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"futurism","text":"Prewrath writers answer that the aorist 'has come' (elthen) in 6:17 announces wrath arriving at that point, and that the Lamb's opening of the seals no more makes them wrath than God's sovereignty makes all suffering wrath. They also note that Jesus' own order (tribulation cut short, cosmic signs, gathering of the elect; Matthew 24:22, 29-31) matches Revelation 6-7, where a great multitude 'coming out of the great tribulation' appears in heaven right after the sixth seal (7:9-14).","passages":["Revelation 6:17","Revelation 7:9-14","Matthew 24:22"],"sources":["kurschner-antichrist-before-dol","rosenthal-prewrath"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"fathers-chiliasm-augustine","title":"The early Fathers, chiliasm and Augustine","question":"Was premillennialism (chiliasm) the consensus of the earliest church, displaced only later by Augustine?","topic":"church-history","summary":"Chiliasm is clearly attested in Papias, Justin Martyr, Irenaeus and Tertullian. Premillennialists see it as the earliest view; Charles Hill and other amillennialists argue a non-chiliast stream was equally early; Augustine's City of God XX shaped Western exegesis for a millennium. All sides agree Scripture, not the Fathers, is decisive.","positions":["histpremil","amil","postmil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"histpremil","text":"Historic premillennialists point out that chiliasm, belief in an earthly thousand-year kingdom after Christ's return, is the earliest clearly attested millennial view. Papias (reported by Irenaeus and Eusebius), Justin Martyr (Dialogue with Trypho 80-81), Irenaeus (Against Heresies 5.32-36) and Tertullian taught it, and Irenaeus traced it to elders who had known John. Opposition came later, chiefly from Alexandrian allegorists such as Origen, and was consolidated by Augustine's City of God XX, so premillennialists see their view as a recovery of the early church's hope.","passages":["Revelation 20:4-6"],"sources":["justin-dialogue-trypho","irenaeus-against-heresies","deere-premil-rev20","collins-were-fathers-amil"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Charles Hill's Regnum Caelorum argues that a non-chiliast stream was early and widespread, visible in writers such as Clement of Rome, Polycarp, Ignatius and the Epistle to Diognetus. Justin himself concedes that many Christians 'of pure and godly' mind did not share his view (Dialogue 80). Hill links chiliasm to the belief that souls wait in an underworld until the resurrection; those who expected to be with Christ in heaven at death (Philippians 1:23, 2 Corinthians 5:8) had no need of an earthly interim kingdom, so there were two coherent early patterns, not one consensus.","passages":["Philippians 1:23","2 Corinthians 5:8"],"sources":["hill-regnum-caelorum","justin-dialogue-trypho","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"Brian Collins, evaluating Hill in Bibliotheca Sacra, argues that the claimed link between chiliasm and an underworld intermediate state fails: Irenaeus taught a millennium while also allowing a heavenly intermediate state. Many of Hill's non-chiliast witnesses are arguments from silence, whereas the chiliast witnesses are explicit. Some premillennialists also read Justin's concession as less sweeping than Hill does, since he immediately condemns those who deny the resurrection of the flesh.","passages":[],"sources":["collins-were-fathers-amil","justin-dialogue-trypho"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Defenders of Hill reply that Dialogue 80 distinguishes godly Christians who disagree from the heretics Justin then condemns, and that silence across several early writers who do discuss the end is significant. They add that later amillennialism did not need Augustine to invent it: Augustine had himself once held a chiliast view and changed his mind on exegetical grounds that he sets out in City of God XX.","passages":[],"sources":["hill-regnum-caelorum","augustine-city-of-god"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists (and others) argue that early prevalence is not decisive. Second-century chiliasm was often bound up with material expectations, such as Papias' description of fantastically fruitful vines, that modern premillennialists do not adopt, and doctrine rests on Scripture rather than early consensus. Augustine's reading of Revelation 20 as Christ's present reign shaped Western exegesis for a millennium, and, as Iain Murray argues, the Puritan hope of gospel advance grew from that Augustinian framework.","passages":["Revelation 20:1-6"],"sources":["augustine-city-of-god","murray-puritan-hope","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"histpremil","text":"Premillennialists agree Scripture is decisive, but argue that the earliest interpreters, nearest to John's language and world, read Revelation 20 sequentially, which counts against claims that a premillennial reading is a modern novelty. They also distinguish historic premillennialism from both crude chiliasm and modern dispensationalism, so criticisms of second-century excesses do not touch Ladd's version.","passages":[],"sources":["ladd-blessed-hope","blomberg-chung-historic-premil","irenaeus-against-heresies"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists respond that proximity cuts both ways: the same early writers also held views, such as a fixed six-thousand-year world-week, that few modern premillennialists follow, and Eusebius already judged Papias limited in understanding on these matters. They suggest the historical claim be stated modestly: chiliasm was prominent in the second century but not unanimous.","passages":[],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","augustine-city-of-god"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"twentieth-century-postmil","title":"Did the twentieth century refute postmillennialism?","question":"Do the world wars and secularisation of the twentieth century, and postmillennialism's decline, count against it, and what does its later revival show?","topic":"church-history","summary":"Postmillennialism dominated much nineteenth-century American Protestantism, faded after the First World War, and revived from the 1970s among Reformed writers. Premillennialists often read the century as confirming biblical warnings of decline; postmillennialists distinguish their view from the liberal idea of progress; amillennialists warn against reading eschatology off the news.","positions":["postmil","disppremil","amil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists argue that their view's decline in the twentieth century reflected cultural mood and theological liberalism rather than exegetical refutation. Historian James Moorhead argues that among mainline Protestants after 1865 postmillennialism eroded into a general faith in progress and the Social Gospel, losing its supernatural content. Historic Reformed postmillennialism (Edwards, Hodge, Warfield) rested on texts such as the Great Commission and Psalm 110:1, and its revival through Rushdoony, Bahnsen, Gentry and Mathison, together with rapid church growth in Africa, Asia and Latin America, indicates in their view that it remains a live exegetical option.","passages":["Matthew 28:18-20","Psalm 110:1","Psalm 22:27"],"sources":["moorhead-erosion-postmil","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","mathison-postmillennialism","murray-puritan-hope"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"disppremil","text":"Premillennial critics argue that the world wars, the Holocaust, totalitarian regimes and the secularisation of the historically Christian West were widely and reasonably taken as counting against postmillennial optimism. They see this as more than a change of mood: Scripture warns of worsening conditions, apostasy and a final tribulation (Matthew 24:21, 2 Thessalonians 2:3). Walvoord regarded postmillennialism as largely abandoned by mid-century, while premillennialism grew through Bible institutes and prophecy conferences, as historian Timothy Weber documents.","passages":["Matthew 24:21","2 Thessalonians 2:3"],"sources":["walvoord-millennial-kingdom","weber-shadow-second-coming","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists answer that their eschatology is a claim about the long-term outcome of history, not any single century; Boettner and Gentry note that its timescale may be measured in millennia. They point to the growth of Christianity in sub-Saharan Africa and elsewhere from a small minority in 1900 to hundreds of millions today, and argue that attempts to match current events to prophecy have themselves produced many failed predictions.","passages":["Daniel 2:35","Isaiah 9:7"],"sources":["boettner-millennium","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Critics reply that if any setback can be absorbed by lengthening the timescale, the view becomes hard to test, and that Christianity's growth in the Global South has coincided with steep decline in former Christendom, which fits an expectation of mixed conditions better than progressive Christianisation. Many premillennialists also reject date-setting and 'newspaper exegesis' as abuses of their own view.","passages":[],"sources":["weber-shadow-second-coming","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists such as Hoekema and Riddlebarger argue that both postmillennial optimism and premillennial expectations of decline err when they treat history as a scorecard. The New Testament promises both gospel advance to all nations and tribulation throughout the age (Matthew 24:14, Acts 14:22), so neither world wars nor revivals measure the truth of an eschatology. The decline and revival of postmillennialism are sociological facts that settle nothing exegetically.","passages":["Matthew 24:14","Acts 14:22"],"sources":["hoekema-bible-future","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennialists agree that history is not the test of exegesis, which is their own point against the premillennial critique, but argue that amillennialism underweights texts promising that the nations will serve Christ before the end (Psalm 72:8-11, Isaiah 2:2-4, 1 Corinthians 15:25). These describe outcomes in history while sin and death still exist, which they say cannot be relocated to the new creation.","passages":["Psalm 72:8-11","Isaiah 2:2-4","1 Corinthians 15:25"],"sources":["gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","bahnsen-victory-in-jesus"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists answer that these promises are fulfilled as the nations are gathered into the church now and are consummated when the kings of the earth bring their glory into the new Jerusalem (Revelation 21:24-26). In their view the New Testament's use of these texts does not envision a golden age before the consummation.","passages":["Revelation 21:24-26"],"sources":["riddlebarger-case-amil","storms-kingdom-come"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"full-preterism-resurrection","title":"Full preterism and the future resurrection","question":"Were the Second Coming, resurrection and final judgment all fulfilled by AD 70, or does the creedal hope of a future bodily resurrection stand?","topic":"final-judgment","summary":"Full (consistent) preterism, or covenant eschatology, holds that all prophecy, including the parousia and resurrection, was fulfilled by AD 70. Partial preterists, amillennialists, postmillennialists and premillennialists reject this, and it stands outside historic creedal orthodoxy because the ecumenical creeds confess a future return and resurrection of the body; proponents reply that the creeds must be tested by Scripture.","positions":["full-preterism","partial-preterism","amil","postmil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"full-preterism","text":"Full preterists (covenant eschatology; Max King, Don Preston) argue that if the time texts are taken at face value, every prophesied end-time event, including the parousia, resurrection and judgment, must have occurred by AD 70. They read the resurrection of Daniel 12:2 and 1 Corinthians 15 as the corporate, covenantal raising of God's people from the death of the old covenant into new covenant life, completed when the temple fell; Daniel 12:7 ties these events to the shattering of 'the power of the holy people'. They see their view as consistent preterism, applying to all prophecy what partial preterists apply to some.","passages":["Daniel 12:1-7","1 Corinthians 15:51-52","Matthew 16:27-28"],"sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","preston-resurrection-daniel-12"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Partial preterists such as Mathison and Gentry argue the New Testament distinguishes AD 70 from a future, visible, bodily return: Jesus will come 'in the same way' as he was seen going (Acts 1:11); the body sown perishable is raised imperishable (1 Corinthians 15:42-44); and our mortal bodies will be given life and redeemed (Romans 8:11, 8:23). Paul condemned Hymenaeus and Philetus for saying 'the resurrection has already happened' (2 Timothy 2:17-18). Mathison's edited volume argues these texts cannot be reduced to a covenantal transition.","passages":["Acts 1:11","1 Corinthians 15:42-44","Romans 8:23","2 Timothy 2:17-18"],"sources":["mathison-when-shall","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"full-preterism","text":"Full preterists respond that 'in the same way' in Acts 1:11 refers to the manner (in a cloud, then hidden from sight) rather than physical visibility to all, and that the 'body' (soma) of 1 Corinthians 15 is corporate, as in 'the body of Christ' (1 Corinthians 12:27). They read Hymenaeus' error as claiming the resurrection was already complete before its appointed time, which Paul could reject while still expecting it within that generation.","passages":["Acts 1:11","1 Corinthians 12:27"],"sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","preston-resurrection-daniel-12"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Critics answer that 1 Corinthians 15 is about individual bodies: the resurrection of 'the dead' is patterned on Christ's own bodily resurrection as firstfruits (15:20-23), and what is sown is the individual mortal body that must 'put on' immortality (15:42-44, 53). If the firstfruits was bodily, the harvest must be of the same kind. They also note that Paul gives no hint that Hymenaeus' error was merely premature.","passages":["1 Corinthians 15:20-23","1 Corinthians 15:53"],"sources":["mathison-when-shall","sproul-last-days"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Critics across the millennial positions note that the ecumenical creeds confess that Christ 'will come again' to judge the living and the dead, together with the resurrection of the body (Nicene Creed; Westminster Confession 32-33). Full preterism therefore stands outside historic creedal orthodoxy; Mathison and others regard it as a serious departure, since the church has never understood these articles as fulfilled in AD 70. They add that texts such as 1 Thessalonians 4:16 and John 5:28-29 describe a public event in which the dead leave their tombs.","passages":["1 Thessalonians 4:16","John 5:28-29"],"sources":["nicene-creed","westminster-confession","mathison-when-shall"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"full-preterism","text":"Proponents respond that the creeds are subordinate standards to be tested by Scripture, a principle the Westminster Confession itself states (1.10), and that the Reformation also overturned long-standing consensus. They argue that creedal language of a future coming was formulated by largely Gentile churches that had lost sight of the Jewish covenantal context of AD 70, and ask to be answered exegetically rather than by appeal to tradition.","passages":[],"sources":["king-spirit-of-prophecy","preston-resurrection-daniel-12"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Critics reply that the Reformers revised doctrines they judged unbiblical but explicitly reaffirmed the ancient creeds' articles on Christ's return and the resurrection. They add that early post-apostolic writers, some writing after AD 70 (for example 1 Clement and the Didache), still expected a future coming and resurrection, which counts against the claim that the first audience understood these events as already fulfilled.","passages":[],"sources":["mathison-when-shall","nicene-creed"],"children":[]}]}]}]}},{"id":"daniel-70th-week-gap","title":"Daniel's seventieth week: a gap or continuous?","question":"Is there a gap between the sixty-ninth and seventieth weeks of Daniel 9, placing the seventieth week in the future?","topic":"seventy-weeks","summary":"Dispensationalists see a gap of the church age between the sixty-ninth and seventieth weeks, with the seventieth week a future seven-year tribulation under the Antichrist. Partial preterists and postmillennialists read the weeks as continuous, fulfilled in Christ and the AD 70 judgment; many amillennialists, following Kline, read the seventy weeks symbolically, reaching to the consummation.","positions":["disppremil","partial-preterism","postmil","amil"],"root":{"id":"claim","kind":"claim","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists (Walvoord, Pentecost, Hoehner) see a gap between the sixty-ninth and seventieth weeks of Daniel 9:24-27. The sixty-nine weeks (483 years) run from Artaxerxes' decree to the Messiah; Hoehner calculated 444 BC to AD 33 using 360-day prophetic years. Verse 26 places two events 'after' the sixty-two weeks (the Messiah cut off, and the city destroyed by 'the people of the prince who is to come') without placing them in the seventieth week. The 'he' who makes a firm covenant for one week (9:27) is that coming prince, the Antichrist, whose mid-week sacrilege matches the abomination of desolation Jesus placed in the future (Matthew 24:15, 2 Thessalonians 2:4).","passages":["Daniel 9:24-27","Matthew 24:15","2 Thessalonians 2:4"],"sources":["hoehner-chronological-aspects","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"obj-1","kind":"objection","position":"partial-preterism","text":"Gentry and other preterists argue that the seventy weeks are a continuous 490-year unit: nothing in the text signals a gap, and inserting two thousand years into a number designed to measure time defeats the measurement. The 'he' of 9:27 most naturally refers to the Messiah, the main figure of 9:26, who confirms the covenant (compare 'the blood of the covenant', Matthew 26:28) and by his death in the middle of the week brings sacrifice to an end (Hebrews 10:8-18). The desolation of Jerusalem in AD 70 is the decreed consequence announced in 9:26-27.","passages":["Daniel 9:26-27","Matthew 26:28","Hebrews 10:8-18"],"sources":["gentry-structure-70-weeks","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion","demar-last-days-madness"],"children":[{"id":"reb-1","kind":"rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists reply that the nearest antecedent of 'he' in 9:27 is 'the prince who is to come' (9:26), and that the verb (higbir) means to 'make strong' or enforce a covenant, suggesting a treaty rather than the new covenant. The six goals of 9:24, including 'to bring in everlasting righteousness' and 'to anoint a most holy place', have not been realised for Daniel's people and city, so the seventieth week must be future. Prophetic gaps occur elsewhere, as in Isaiah 61:1-2 read in Luke 4:18-21.","passages":["Daniel 9:24","Isaiah 61:1-2","Luke 4:18-21"],"sources":["hoehner-chronological-aspects","walvoord-millennial-kingdom","pentecost-things-to-come"],"children":[{"id":"cr-1","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"postmil","text":"Postmillennial preterists answer that 'prince' in 9:26 stands in a dependent phrase ('the people of the prince'), so the subject of that clause is the people, and the Messiah remains the passage's central figure. They read the six goals of 9:24 (finishing transgression, atoning for iniquity, bringing everlasting righteousness) as accomplished in Christ's cross and the new covenant (Hebrews 9:26, Romans 3:21-26).","passages":["Hebrews 9:26","Romans 3:21-26"],"sources":["gentry-structure-70-weeks","gentry-he-shall-have-dominion"],"children":[]}]}]},{"id":"obj-2","kind":"objection","position":"amil","text":"Kline and many amillennialists read the seventy weeks as a symbolic sabbatical period (seventy sevens, echoing the jubilee pattern of Leviticus 25 and the sabbath-years of 2 Chronicles 36:21) rather than a precise count of 490 years. The seventieth week begins with the Messiah's covenant-confirming work and extends symbolically through the church age to the consummation, with the desolator representing hostile powers until the decreed end. Riddlebarger argues this avoids both an arbitrary gap and the need to compress all fulfilment into the first century.","passages":["Leviticus 25:8-10","2 Chronicles 36:21","Daniel 9:2"],"sources":["kline-covenant-seventieth-week","riddlebarger-seventieth-week","riddlebarger-case-amil"],"children":[{"id":"reb-2","kind":"rebuttal","position":"disppremil","text":"Dispensationalists reply that Daniel's prayer concerns Jeremiah's literal seventy years (Daniel 9:2, Jeremiah 25:11-12), so the answer of seventy 'sevens' naturally continues a literal reckoning. Hoehner's calculation landing on Jesus' final entry into Jerusalem suggests the sixty-nine weeks were exact, so the seventieth will be too. A symbolic week stretching across two thousand years, they argue, is a larger gap than the dispensational one.","passages":["Daniel 9:2","Jeremiah 25:11-12"],"sources":["hoehner-chronological-aspects","walvoord-millennial-kingdom"],"children":[{"id":"cr-2","kind":"counter-rebuttal","position":"amil","text":"Amillennialists answer that conservative calculations of the sixty-nine weeks vary widely depending on which decree and calendar are chosen, and that Hoehner's scheme relies on a 360-day year the text does not specify. Kline argues that Daniel 9, saturated with the vocabulary of the Mosaic covenant, points to theological rather than arithmetical precision.","passages":["Daniel 9:4-19"],"sources":["kline-covenant-seventieth-week","riddlebarger-seventieth-week"],"children":[]}]}]}]}}]`),i=t,s=new Map(i.map(e=>[e.id,e]));export{i as a,s as b};
